"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[495],{6495:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2015-09-06T16:10:00","modified":"2018-01-03T12:27:00","id":1024,"title":"Depreciating GitHub Repos -  A Suggestion","article":"<p><strong>Update 2018-01-03</strong> GitHub now allows for projects to be archived in settings. Which allows projects to be viewable but not committed too. </p>\\n<p><a href=\\"https://help.github.com/en/github/creating-cloning-and-archiving-repositories/archiving-repositories\\">https://help.github.com/en/github/creating-cloning-and-archiving-repositories/archiving-repositories</a></p>\\n<h2 id=\\"original-article\\">Original Article</h2>\\n<p>Sorry to break the bad news at this moment in time there is no specific way of depreciating a repo other than simply deleting it.</p>\\n<p>Deleting a repo is not a sensible option however, lots of things link into public repos these days such as bower. If we simply deleted the repo, then people that rely on it being there and have forked or cloned the repo will not be very happy &amp; letting them down might mean they lose faith in using to use your code in future.</p>\\n<p>There is currently an on going discussion about this on GitHub but no response from <a href=\\"https://github.com/isaacs/github/issues/144\\">GitHub</a> themselves.</p>\\n<p>You could, and in fact should, state on the Readme file that the project has been depreciated and describe whether you are moving to a new project or leaving it completely etc. This might not be immediately obvious to new people or even current watchers that you have depreciated it though. And it is not useful if you are using the GitHub API to list your own repos on another site/ui etc.</p>\\n<p>Until GitHub offers us something better, I have been renaming my repos and placing -DEPRECATED- at the beginning of the repo name. GitHib creates a type of symlink in the background to the original repo name so if someone is cloning via the original url or tries to go to the project it still links correctly. This provides your users with a way of still accessing the repo and its history, but making sure others know it is not being worked on any more.</p>\\n<p>On my web site I list my current repos via a request to the GitHub search API and having the &quot;-DEPRECATED-&quot; at the beginning allows me to exclude them from the results. Although this is a long way from a perfect solution it does work.</p>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["github","git","source control"]}')}}]);